  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A composite parallel plate capacitor is made up of two different dielectric materials with different thickness (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>) as shown in figure. The two different dielectric material are separated by a conducting foil <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi></math>. The voltage of the conducting foil is _____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0062_1459f8e23699.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 120px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "60",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0062_1459f8e23699.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 120px;\" /></p><p>Capacitance of each capacitor&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>&#1013;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>&#1013;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></p><p>Here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;area of plate.</p><p>Equivalent capacitance is&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>24</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>&#1013;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></p><p>Now, net charge stored is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>net</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi>eq</mi></msub><mfenced><mi>&#916;V</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>24</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mfenced><mn>100</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>240</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></p><p>Potential drop across&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#916;</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>240</mn><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math></p><p>Thus, voltage&nbsp;of the conducting foil is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>foil</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
}